// Store buffering over non-atomic accesses. MoRDor's own test.
//
// sMRD's language has no non-atomic accesses: every load and store carries one
// of rlx, rel, acq and sc. MoRDor accepts na and, under sMRD, treats it as
// relaxed: it reports no data race. RC11 makes a race on a non-atomic location
// undefined behaviour, so the same program has undefined behaviour under RC11
// and none under sMRD. Both allow the store-buffering outcome.
x := 0; y := 0;
{
  x.store(1, na);
  r1 := y.load(na)
} ||| {
  y.store(1, na);
  r2 := x.load(na)
}
%%
allow (r1 = 0 && r2 = 0) [sMRD, RC11]
forbid (ub) [sMRD]
allow (ub) [RC11]
